{
 "cluster": "c04",
 "genre_note": "Cluster c04 is 20 business/finance news articles (26,798 words) from 16 publishers, spanning market wraps, single-company earnings, interview Q&As, policy commentary, a trade-press analyst report, a court-case report and one science press release that ran on a business wire. Decomposition yielded 137 notes across 19 sub-plans, with 96.2% of source words assigned (verified with scripts/verify_cluster_plan.py: PASS on block ranges, no duplicate source, one BB per note, no note over the 1,800-word ceiling, all sub-plans in the 4-15 range).\n\nBuilding-block distribution matched the prediction that news is dominated by empirical_observation (~80 notes) and concept (~14). Beyond those: model (~24) for causal/mechanism content (Wnt/Rnf43/Daam1 signalling; capital rotation out of China into Japan; the go-shop clause's matching right and its knock-on effect on the AGM date; Nike's DTC-vs-wholesale channel economics; Israeli war-to-deficit transmission channels); argument (13) where a claim is stated with grounds (the DBM's case for a surplus budget, Leiderman's \"no sacred numbers\" deficit argument, the Reliance SOTP buy case, Hollenbeck on gender parity and returns); counter_argument (7), which occurred more often than expected because several documents are structured as claim-and-rebuttal (Kyncl's AdWords/ad-agency rebuttal to the \"labels are obsolete\" thesis; Believe CEO Ladegaillerie contradicting Kyncl on whether AI-detection tooling is ready; the \"Mossad foreknew the attack\" counter-reading of the October 7 intelligence-failure consensus; Volker's argument that a Trump presidency would not doom Ukraine; Trump's disclaimer defence and Engoron's rejection of it); procedure (3) only where genuinely ordered (AfroFuture's Rising Star Challenge entry-to-final pipeline, Leiderman's proposed rehabilitation-planning steps, IoT Analytics' vendor/adopter question checklists).\n\nhypothesis is essentially absent, as expected: exactly one note (Kyncl's dated, falsifiable prediction that music will feel AI's impact before other industries \"within the next year\"). Forward-looking material elsewhere \u2014 the Ukraine 2024 outlook, India's 2030 GDP path, the earnings-week consensus estimates \u2014 is reported speech about forecasts, so it was classified empirical_observation rather than manufactured into hypotheses. No navigation notes were produced.\n\nTwo decomposition pressures dominated. First, roundup documents: doc_0338 is one \"earnings week ahead\" article but eight unrelated company previews, and doc_0028/doc_0357 are ASX market wraps mixing local single-stock moves, US index moves, Fed policy and a stray corporate-governance quote \u2014 these were split at company/subject boundaries, never at word counts. Second, shared entities: the Federal Reserve rate-path note draws blocks from both ASX wraps (doc_0028 and doc_0357), and two Trump property notes (Mar-a-Lago, the Fifth Avenue penthouse) draw from both the ruling report (doc_0529) and the Bloomberg valuation piece (doc_0530) rather than splitting that evidence across near-duplicate notes.\n\nDropped material is 3.8% of words and is almost entirely genuine chrome: headlines, section headers, bylines, photo credits, \"Also Read\" links, the Guardian's 89-word Football Daily newsletter promotion (doc_0089), the MBW/JKBX and TuneCore sponsor blurbs (doc_0455, doc_0085), and verbatim pull-quote repeats of text already assigned (doc_0455 blocks 23/39/47, doc_0085 blocks 13/37). Two documents land below 90%: doc_0089 at 88.7% (the newsletter promo alone is 9% of that article) and doc_0455 at 89.7% (sponsor blurb plus three duplicated pull quotes). Both are honest promotional/duplicate losses, not omitted substance.\n\nOne block that looked droppable was kept: doc_0215 block 39 is a correction notice, and it restates the load-bearing figure of the article's own headline claim (\"Nike's gross margin fell 0.1 percentage points\"), so it was assigned to the gross-margin note rather than deleted as boilerplate. Q&A question headers in the two interview pieces (doc_0085, doc_0125) were also assigned rather than dropped, since they carry the framing that makes each answer retrievable.",
